/*
Problem: Move Zeroes (LC 283)
Topic: Array / Two Pointers

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Move all non-zero elements to the
front while maintaining their
relative order.

After placing every non-zero element,
fill the remaining positions with
zeros.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Initialize:

   lastNonZeroFoundAt = 0

2. Traverse the array.

3. If the current element is non-zero:

   Place it at index
   lastNonZeroFoundAt.

   Increment lastNonZeroFoundAt.

4. After processing all elements,
   fill the remaining positions
   with zeros.

5. The array is modified in-place.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[0,1,0,3,12]

After moving non-zeros:

[1,3,12,3,12]

Fill remaining positions with zeros:

[1,3,12,0,0]

Answer:

[1,3,12,0,0]

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[0,0,1]

After moving non-zeros:

[1,0,1]

Fill remaining positions:

[1,0,0]

Answer:

[1,0,0]

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Every non-zero element is copied to
the next available position.

After all non-zero elements are
placed, every remaining position
must contain zero.

The relative order of non-zero
elements is preserved.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Each element is visited once.

Space : O(1)

The array is modified in-place.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Two Pointers / Array
*/

class Solution {
public:
    void moveZeroes(vector<int>& nums) {

        int lastNonZeroFoundAt = 0;

        // Move all non-zero elements
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {

            if (nums[i] != 0) {

                nums[lastNonZeroFoundAt] = nums[i];
                lastNonZeroFoundAt++;
            }
        }

        // Fill remaining positions with zeros
        for (int i = lastNonZeroFoundAt;
             i < nums.size();
             i++) {

            nums[i] = 0;
        }
    }
};
